course: dictionary
lesson: 15287
title: One at a time
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Singly is an adverb, a word that tells you how something is done. It means one at a time, or one by one, not in a group. «Please enter singly.» The shop wants people to come in one after another, not all together.
explain: Singly can also mean alone, on its own. «Some spiders live singly, but ants live in big groups.» Here singly means each spider lives by itself. Look at the words around it to tell which meaning fits.
explain: Test your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If «one at a time» or «on its own» makes sense, you have the right meaning. Singly often appears with or, as in «singly or in pairs», which means some alone and some in twos.
fact: Singly means one at a time, or one by one.
fact: Singly can also mean alone, on its own.
fact: «Sold singly» means you can buy one item on its own, not in a pack.
fact: Singly is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens.
exercise: «Please enter singly.» What does singly mean here? | *One at a time | Very loudly | All together in a big crowd | Without any shoes
exercise: Pens are sold «singly or in packs». What does singly mean in this sentence? | In a big box with lots of others | *On its own, one pen at a time | Very cheaply | Forgotten
exercise: «Eggs are sold singly at 20p each.» How much would 3 eggs cost if you buy them singly? | 3p | 23p | *60p | 20p
test:
«Some spiders live singly, while ants live in big groups.» What does singly suggest? | They live together in one nest | They live in the water | *Each spider lives alone | They live in trees only
test:
«The children crossed the bridge singly.» Which word means the same as singly? | *One by one | Loudly | Together | Quickly
exam:
«The players came onto the pitch singly.» How did the players come onto the pitch? | Together in a huge group | Backwards | *One by one | Slowly and sadly
